Fix the eMMC read speed wall properly.  0112 showed that skipping the GCC apps
branch kick in oe_ensure_clock() turns ~15 KiB/s into ~3 MB/s, but a boot with
the kick fully disabled can lose commands after a long idle gap and panic in
IOFindBSDRoot ("ONC eMMC root emmcroot0 unavailable").  The branch is only
hardware-gated when the core has been idle, and a kick after such an idle gap
takes ~32 ms - that is what made every command slow.

So: kick the branch only when the previous command ended more than 10 ms ago
(real idle gap, or the first command after init), and force a kick on the
retry path in oe_read_lba().  A steady command stream then runs at bus speed
while idle gaps still re-enable the clock.

  * emmcroot_nobranch=1   - skip the kick entirely (A/B, as in 0112)
  * emmcroot_kickalways=1 - old kick-per-command behaviour (safety fallback)
  * emmcroot_rdprof still prints "prof branch=<us>" while profiling

Base: tmp/onc_emmc_after_0112.c (= CI main after 0112).
--- a/bsd/dev/onc_emmc.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/onc_emmc.c
